1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,293
In September 2000, three men from a small,

2
00:00:03,293 --> 00:00:06,498
money-losing startup flew to Dallas with an offer.

3
00:00:06,948 --> 00:00:11,232
Buy us for fifty million dollars, they said, and we'll run your business online.

4
00:00:11,682 --> 00:00:13,172
The startup was Netflix.

5
00:00:13,172 --> 00:00:14,289
The answer was no.

6
00:00:14,739 --> 00:00:16,039
Everyone knows that part.

7
00:00:16,039 --> 00:00:20,095
What most people don't know is that Blockbuster fought back, and for a moment,

8
00:00:20,095 --> 00:00:20,875
it was winning.

9
00:00:21,325 --> 00:00:23,135
This is Built and Broken.

10
00:00:23,135 --> 00:00:24,149
Episode three.

11
00:00:24,149 --> 00:00:25,018
Blockbuster.

12
00:00:26,118 --> 00:00:28,405
On the nineteenth of October 1985,

13
00:00:28,405 --> 00:00:33,989
a businessman named David Cook opened the first Blockbuster store in Dallas, Texas.

14
00:00:34,439 --> 00:00:37,263
Most video shops back then were small and cramped.

15
00:00:37,263 --> 00:00:39,070
Cook's store was big and bright,

16
00:00:39,070 --> 00:00:43,362
with around eight thousand tapes on the shelves and a computerized checkout.

17
00:00:43,812 --> 00:00:50,378
In 1987, an entrepreneur named Wayne Huizenga took control and did what he did best.

18
00:00:50,378 --> 00:00:51,317
He expanded.

19
00:00:51,317 --> 00:00:51,707
Fast.

20
00:00:52,157 --> 00:00:55,327
Blockbuster spread across America and then the world.

21
00:00:55,327 --> 00:00:57,061
Friday night meant one thing.

22
00:00:57,061 --> 00:01:00,889
Walk the aisles, pick a movie, grab some popcorn at the counter.

23
00:01:01,339 --> 00:01:07,228
In 1994, the media giant Viacom bought Blockbuster in a deal worth eight point four

24
00:01:07,228 --> 00:01:08,363
billion dollars.

25
00:01:09,463 --> 00:01:11,878
By 2004, Blockbuster was at its peak.

26
00:01:11,878 --> 00:01:13,901
More than nine thousand stores.

27
00:01:13,901 --> 00:01:15,924
Eighty four thousand employees.

28
00:01:15,924 --> 00:01:19,710
Six point one billion dollars in revenue in a single year.

29
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:24,039
But there was a secret inside that business, and every customer knew it.

30
00:01:24,039 --> 00:01:24,578
Late fees.

31
00:01:25,028 --> 00:01:27,365
Return a tape a day late, and you paid.

32
00:01:27,365 --> 00:01:32,220
In 2000, Blockbuster collected nearly eight hundred million dollars in late fees.

33
00:01:32,670 --> 00:01:35,249
That was about sixteen percent of its revenue.

34
00:01:35,249 --> 00:01:38,667
One dollar in every six came from customers making a mistake.

35
00:01:39,117 --> 00:01:40,216
Remember that number.

36
00:01:40,216 --> 00:01:44,399
A business that makes money when its customers are unhappy is a business waiting

37
00:01:44,399 --> 00:01:45,288
for a competitor.

38
00:01:46,388 --> 00:01:48,803
That competitor started in 1997.

39
00:01:48,803 --> 00:01:54,162
Netflix rented DVDs by mail, and, eventually, with no late fees at all.

40
00:01:54,612 --> 00:01:58,794
By 2000, the dot-com bubble had burst, and Netflix was losing money.

41
00:01:58,794 --> 00:02:01,808
So its founders, Reed Hastings and Marc Randolph,

42
00:02:01,808 --> 00:02:05,253
with their finance chief Barry McCarthy, went to Dallas.

43
00:02:05,703 --> 00:02:09,514
Their pitch: Netflix would become Blockbuster's online business.

44
00:02:09,514 --> 00:02:11,479
The price, fifty million dollars.

45
00:02:11,929 --> 00:02:16,215
Marc Randolph later wrote that Blockbuster's chief executive, John Antioco,

46
00:02:16,215 --> 00:02:17,815
was struggling not to laugh.

47
00:02:18,265 --> 00:02:19,682
McCarthy put it more bluntly.

48
00:02:19,682 --> 00:02:21,441
They laughed us out of their office.

49
00:02:21,891 --> 00:02:25,568
To be fair, it looked like a sensible decision at the time.

50
00:02:25,568 --> 00:02:27,687
Netflix was tiny and unprofitable.

51
00:02:27,687 --> 00:02:29,682
Blockbuster was making billions.

52
00:02:30,132 --> 00:02:33,653
Netflix flew home and decided to beat them instead.

53
00:02:33,653 --> 00:02:37,174
By the end of 2006, it had six million subscribers.

54
00:02:38,274 --> 00:02:40,620
Here is the part that usually gets left out.

55
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:42,912
Blockbuster did not ignore Netflix forever.

56
00:02:43,362 --> 00:02:46,079
In 2004 it launched Blockbuster Online.

57
00:02:46,079 --> 00:02:50,398
And on the first of January 2005, it did something remarkable.

58
00:02:50,398 --> 00:02:51,931
It scrapped late fees.

59
00:02:52,381 --> 00:02:53,348
It was expensive.

60
00:02:53,348 --> 00:02:57,498
By one estimate, ending late fees cost about two hundred million dollars,

61
00:02:57,498 --> 00:03:01,478
and launching the online business cost about two hundred million more.

62
00:03:01,928 --> 00:03:07,214
Then, in late 2006, Blockbuster played the one card Netflix could never match.

63
00:03:07,214 --> 00:03:07,960
Its stores.

64
00:03:08,410 --> 00:03:09,970
It was called Total Access.

65
00:03:09,970 --> 00:03:12,397
Rent a DVD online, and when you were done,

66
00:03:12,397 --> 00:03:17,020
you could return it to any Blockbuster store and walk out with another movie for

67
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:17,309
free.

68
00:03:17,759 --> 00:03:18,934
Customers loved it.

69
00:03:18,934 --> 00:03:22,893
Total Access passed two million subscribers, and inside Netflix,

70
00:03:22,893 --> 00:03:24,130
people were worried.

71
00:03:24,130 --> 00:03:27,408
Blockbuster was spending heavily, and it was working.

72
00:03:28,508 --> 00:03:30,389
And then Blockbuster turned on itself.

73
00:03:30,839 --> 00:03:34,763
The fight with Netflix was costing money, and the company was in debt.

74
00:03:34,763 --> 00:03:39,135
The activist investor Carl Icahn, who held a large stake and sat on the board,

75
00:03:39,135 --> 00:03:40,481
questioned the spending.

76
00:03:40,931 --> 00:03:45,052
Antioco clashed with the board over his pay, and in 2007 he left.

77
00:03:45,502 --> 00:03:47,283
His replacement was Jim Keyes,

78
00:03:47,283 --> 00:03:50,607
the former boss of the convenience store chain 7-Eleven.

79
00:03:50,607 --> 00:03:53,160
He believed the future was still in stores.

80
00:03:53,610 --> 00:03:55,109
Money was running short.

81
00:03:55,109 --> 00:03:59,855
Marketing for the online service had already been cut back, and under Keyes,

82
00:03:59,855 --> 00:04:02,041
Total Access became more expensive.

83
00:04:02,041 --> 00:04:03,665
Subscriber growth stalled.

84
00:04:04,115 --> 00:04:06,485
Blockbuster had built a real answer to Netflix,

85
00:04:06,485 --> 00:04:08,503
and then it took its foot off the pedal.

86
00:04:09,603 --> 00:04:11,971
Meanwhile, the ground was moving.

87
00:04:11,971 --> 00:04:15,129
In January 2007, Netflix launched streaming.

88
00:04:15,129 --> 00:04:19,937
Just a thousand films at first, but no disc, no envelope, no store.

89
00:04:20,387 --> 00:04:22,867
And from the other side came Redbox.

90
00:04:22,867 --> 00:04:27,483
Red kiosks outside supermarkets, renting DVDs for a dollar a night.

91
00:04:27,483 --> 00:04:31,823
By 2008, Redbox had more locations in America than Blockbuster.

92
00:04:32,273 --> 00:04:36,762
Blockbuster was squeezed between the convenience of streaming and the price of a

93
00:04:36,762 --> 00:04:37,659
vending machine.

94
00:04:37,659 --> 00:04:41,475
Its stores, once its greatest strength, were now a giant fixed cost.

95
00:04:41,925 --> 00:04:46,801
On the twenty third of September 2010, Blockbuster filed for bankruptcy.

96
00:04:47,251 --> 00:04:50,722
That same year, Netflix passed twenty million subscribers.

97
00:04:51,172 --> 00:04:56,147
In 2011, the satellite TV company Dish bought what was left of Blockbuster at

98
00:04:56,147 --> 00:04:59,958
auction for about three hundred and twenty million dollars.

99
00:04:59,958 --> 00:05:03,253
In 2014, Dish closed the last company-owned stores.

100
00:05:03,703 --> 00:05:07,409
By 2019, there was just one Blockbuster left on Earth.

101
00:05:07,409 --> 00:05:11,871
An independently owned store in Bend, Oregon, that kept the name.

102
00:05:12,971 --> 00:05:14,582
Kodak protected its profits.

103
00:05:14,582 --> 00:05:16,367
Nokia measured the wrong thing.

104
00:05:16,817 --> 00:05:18,761
Blockbuster's story is stranger.

105
00:05:18,761 --> 00:05:20,341
It found the right answer.

106
00:05:20,341 --> 00:05:21,252
It paid for it.

107
00:05:21,252 --> 00:05:22,163
It was winning.

108
00:05:22,163 --> 00:05:23,378
And then it stopped.

109
00:05:23,828 --> 00:05:28,096
Fighting a disruptor costs money, and the bill arrives long before the reward.

110
00:05:28,096 --> 00:05:30,394
Blockbuster blinked when the bill arrived.

111
00:05:30,844 --> 00:05:33,755
Fifty million dollars was the cheapest chance it ever had.

112
00:05:33,755 --> 00:05:36,818
Every chance after that cost more, and it took fewer of them.

113
00:05:37,268 --> 00:05:39,096
This has been Built and Broken.

114
00:05:39,096 --> 00:05:43,578
Subscribe for the next story of a giant that saw the future, and still fell.
